<!-- HERO -->
<section class="hero">
  <h1>Low-Cost EKG Signal Acquisition System</h1>

  <p>
    A biomedical engineering project focused on designing a low-cost electrocardiogram (EKG) system capable of capturing
    and processing weak cardiac electrical signals using analog filtering and signal conditioning techniques.
  </p>
</section>

<!-- OVERVIEW -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="card">
    <h2>Project Overview</h2>
    <p>
      Electrocardiograms (EKGs) are widely used for monitoring cardiac health by measuring the electrical activity of the heart.
      While commercial systems are highly accurate, they are often expensive and inaccessible for low-resource or educational settings.
      This project explores a simplified, cost-effective approach to biosignal acquisition.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- PROBLEM -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="card">
    <h2>The Problem</h2>
    <p>
      The heart produces extremely weak electrical signals (millivolt range), making them highly susceptible to noise from:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>Muscle activity and motion artifacts</li>
      <li>Electromagnetic interference (60 Hz noise)</li>
      <li>Poor electrode contact</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      The challenge is to amplify and preserve cardiac signals while removing unwanted noise without increasing system complexity or cost.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- DESIGN -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="card">
    <h2>Design Approach</h2>

    <ul>
      <li>High-pass filter (1 Hz cutoff) to remove baseline drift</li>
      <li>Low-pass filtering to reduce high-frequency noise</li>
      <li>Analog signal conditioning for waveform clarity</li>
      <li>Ground reference via limb electrode (elbow grounding)</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="img-placeholder">
      Circuit / Schematic Image Placeholder
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- CHALLENGES -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="card">
    <h2>Engineering Challenges</h2>
    <p>
      The main difficulty was isolating the weak cardiac signal from surrounding noise.
      Motion artifacts, electrode instability, and environmental interference required careful filter tuning
      to preserve key waveform components such as the P wave, QRS complex, and T wave.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- RESULTS -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="card">
    <h2>Results</h2>
    <p>
      The final system successfully captured recognizable EKG waveforms.
      Filtering significantly reduced baseline drift and high-frequency noise,
      producing a stable signal suitable for visualization and basic cardiac analysis.
    </p>

    <div class="img-placeholder">
